Key observations:

* IDT errors concentrate at the low-T edge (1000–1250 K), where C2/C3
  ignition is sensitive to minor channels; they collapse at tighter eps.
* LBV errors are systematically smaller than IDT errors at the same eps —
  autoignition-sampled DRGEP transfers well to flames.
* Knee behaviour is non-monotone (e.g. tier B eps=0.03 has lower IDT error
  than eps=0.06) — a known property of thresholded graph reduction.
* The largest retained error is for C3H8 ignition at tier A eps=0.15 (3.9 %);
  all other fuels ≤ ~1 % at that eps.

## 5. Experimental validation

Dataset: 560 FFCM-2/SFCPD curated targets (365 shock-tube IDT, 195 LBV)
for the 8 tier fuels + H2+CO syngas (52 HE-diluted targets excluded — helium
absent from the mechanism; no pure-CO targets exist in the set, CO is covered
via syngas). Model setup per target: constant-volume adiabatic IDT with the
matched diagnostic (pressure-based targets → max dP/dt; CO2/OH*/CH* onset
targets → max rise rate of the corresponding species) and freely-propagating
LBV (mixture-averaged transport) at the target's stated T, P, and mole-%
composition.

**Caveats (explicit):** (i) the model IDT diagnostics are matched per target
(pressure-based targets → max dP/dt; CO2/OH*/CH* onset targets → max rise
rate of the corresponding species, with ground-state CH as the CH* proxy) —
a documented proxy for excited-state CH*; (ii) targets are consolidated
multi-study nominals with curated uncertainty, not raw single-series points;
(iii) a CSV-writer bug (`NoneType` join on no-ignition rows) silently
dropped whole shards in the first runs; it was found, fixed, and all
affected cases re-run.

### Parent baseline (reference floor)

| fuel | LBV mean err | LBV within uq | IDT mean |ln ratio| | IDT within uq |
|------|-------------|---------------|------------------------|----------------|
| C2H2 | 0.058 | 5/12 | 0.659 | 5/30 |
| C2H4 | 0.088 | 20/33 | 0.356 | 48/78 |
| C2H6 | 0.083 | 34/55 | 0.697 | 12/70 |
| C3H6 | 0.123 | 12/28 | 0.491 | 28/57 |
| C3H8 | 0.098 | 11/21 | 0.236 | 54/66 |
| CH4  | 0.161 | 7/13 | 0.401 | 28/62 |
| H2   | 0.175 | 10/21 | 0.370 (2 pts) | 0/2 |
| H2+CO| 0.094 | 7/12 | — | — |

IDT error by diagnostic class (parent): dP/dt 0.462, CO2-onset 0.369,
OH-onset 0.460, CH-onset 0.485 (mean |ln ratio|) — uniform across classes,
i.e. no diagnostic-specific bias. Parent IDT error grows at T < 1250 K
(mean 0.62 vs 0.36–0.42 above), and is largest for C2H6/C2H2 ignition and
rich H2 flames. This is the accuracy the parent itself delivers; a reduced
mechanism is "useful" if it stays close to this baseline.

### Candidates vs parent on the experimental set

| candidate | n shared | max Δ vs parent | mean Δ | p95 Δ |
|-----------|----------|-----------------|--------|-------|
| A-eps0.15 (86 sp) | 560 | 0.034 | 0.002 | 0.013 |
| C-eps0.15 (62 sp) | 110 | 0.055 | 0.007 | 0.035 |
| C-eps0.25 (49 sp) | 110 | 0.097 | 0.014 | 0.048 |

Per-fuel experimental error tables for the candidates match the parent's to
±0.01 almost everywhere (see `results/analyze_expfinal_*.txt`); the largest
deviations are high-pressure (73–104 atm) CH4 IDT points, outside the
P ≤ 10 atm reduction domain. The two extreme-lean high-T H2 IDT targets
(φ = 0.03, T > 2000 K) deviate by a factor ~1.4–1.6 for parent *and*
candidates alike — an inherent parent behavior, not a reduction artifact.

**Conclusion:** at all three compression levels, experimental accuracy is
statistically indistinguishable from the parent. The reduction is
experimentally "free"; the only cost is the (smaller) fidelity error.

## 6. Final candidates and trade-off

Three mechanisms are delivered (files under `mechanisms/`), spanning the
domain-breadth × size × accuracy trade-off:

| # | file | species | rxns | domain | IDT max err vs parent | LBV max err vs parent | Δ vs parent on exp. set |
|---|------|---------|------|--------|-----------------------|-----------------------|--------------------------|
| 1 | `USC2_reduced_C0C3_86sp.yaml` | 86 | 613 | C0–C3 (8 fuels) | 3.9 % | 0.7 % | max 3.4 % |
| 2 | `USC2_reduced_C0C1_62sp.yaml` | 62 | 370 | C0–C1 (H2/CO/CH4) | 2.0 % | 2.5 % | max 5.5 % (only P>70 atm) |
| 3 | `USC2_reduced_C0C1_49sp.yaml` | 49 | 290 | C0–C1 (H2/CO/CH4) | 3.2 % | 12.8 % | max 9.7 % |

Reading the trade-off:

* **Domain breadth is the dominant cost.** Compressing C0–C3 (8 fuels incl.
  propane/propene and rich PAH-adjacent channels) bottoms out near ~86
  species at high fidelity, while C0–C1 reaches 62. Below ~50 species the
  flame error grows steeply (2.5 % → 12.8 %) — the classic reduction knee.
* **Reaction network tracks species** at ~7 reactions/species (613/370/290).
* **Accuracy loss is negligible relative to experiment** at all three levels:
  the parent's own experimental scatter exceeds the fidelity error by an
  order of magnitude, so all three are "experimentally equivalent" to USC-II
  within their stated domains.
* Examined and rejected: tier-B (C1–C2) series — dominated by candidate 1
  (smaller and broader); tier-A eps=0.4 (73 sp) — 21 % IDT error at the C3
  low-T edge plus 13.8 % LBV error (lean CH4, 5 atm), beyond "useful";
  tier-C eps=0.4 (43 sp) — 21 % LBV error.

## 7. Limitations and coverage gaps

* **No C4+/aromatics** in the target domain — reduced mechanisms must not be
  applied to butane+, PAH/soot, or low-T (NTC) chemistry.
* **Pressure extrapolation:** fidelity is validated at P ≤ 10 atm; at
  73–104 atm the 62-sp candidate deviates up to 5.5 % from the parent.
* **Diagnostic proxies:** model IDT uses max dP/dt for pressure-diagnostic
  targets and species rise-rates for CO2/OH*/CH* onset targets (ground-state
  CH proxies excited-state CH*). Fidelity comparisons use max dT/dt for both
  models — diagnostic choice cancels there.
* **Sampling:** DRGEP states came from autoignition only (PSR was dropped
  after CVODES failures; 1-D flame sampling judged too costly). Flame-speed
  fidelity was nevertheless verified a posteriori on the full grids
  (160/120/60 cases for tiers A/B/C) and found small.
* **Experimental data** are FFCM-2/SFCPD consolidated targets, not raw
  series; 52 HE-diluted targets excluded; no pure-CO targets in the set.
* Thermo polynomials inherit small discontinuities from the parent (Cantera
  warnings), unchanged by reduction.

